abstract: >-
  An MCP server for professional geoprocessing under a rule the caller cannot
  bend: every geometry and every number comes from a real engine run, never
  from the model. Each operation that writes a dataset also
  writes a manifest beside it — the inputs and their hashes, the exact
  parameters, the coordinate-system decisions and the reason for each, the
  engine and its version, and the deterministic checks that ran — and the
  manifest is written before a failed check raises, so the audit trail survives
  the error. Capabilities are reached through a searchable catalogue rather
  than a wall of tools, because a caller's ability to pick the right tool
  degrades long before a catalogue does. A manifest records what was done; it
  does not certify that what was done was right, and MapSmith says so in its
  own documentation rather than leaving a reader to find out.
